"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[209],{1209:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2016-04-28T12:15:00","modified":"2016-04-28T12:15:00","id":1031,"title":"Javascript Decorators - From The Ground Up","article":"<p>Having looked around at articles covering Decorators in javascript I couldn&#39;t find any that started at the very basic, so thought I would try and fill that gap.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"tldr\\">TL;DR</h2>\\n<p>In ES2016 (aka ES7) decorators are being added and you can now see them being used in examples on the web for popular libraries. All decorators do is extend the behaviour of classes/functions, without modifying them, but by wrapping it to make something new that extends the functionality.</p>\\n<p><em>If coming from Python they are very similar to decorators already in that language, which is the inspiration for the spec in javascript, in .Net there are Attributes which work in a similar manner to decorators except they are resolved at compile time rather than at runtime.</em></p>\\n<h2 id=\\"decorators\\">Decorators</h2>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-class\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span> </span>{\\n\\n    @deprecated\\n    myFunc() {}\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>Decorators can range from doing simple things from declaring a property is readonly, which Ill be showing in this aricle, to dependancy injection, mixins to extend objects, function loggers, arguments checker, obsolete warnings, and so on. There are decorators already in libraries such as Angular and Aurelia, but there is also a core-decorators package available that provides some utility decorators too that are worth looking at, at the very least as examples on how to use decorators.</p>\\n<p><em><strong>Warning:</strong> Although popular libraries such as Angular and Aurelia are already using decorators, they are still in late April 2016 in flux and could change (<strong>update</strong> this is still true in June 2020, although the proposal in stable). This guide shows the original proposal of descriptors being used. I will try to keep this article up to date with those changes, but the general idea being discussed here should be fairly similar to what is mentioned in this article.</em></p>\\n<p><em>If you are using Babel version 6+ you need to install and run a plugin <a href=\\"https://babeljs.io/docs/en/babel-plugin-proposal-decorators\\">babel-plugin-transform-decorators-legacy</a> which is available via <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/babel-plugin-transform-decorators-legacy\\">NPM</a></em>.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"so-why-decorators\\">So, Why Decorators?</h2>\\n<p>Decorators are based around the idea of being able to add consistent repeated functionality to different areas of an application via annotations that &#39;decorate&#39; properties or classes and is an explicit implementation of the Decorator Pattern.</p>\\n<p>Why would we need to? As an example in ES5, as Ill show later, you can make a class property readonly, but doing so is exceptionally verbose and needs to be repeated for every property. Instead we can create a decorator that contains the code for making a property readonly and then &#39;decorate&#39; those properties you want to be readonly with a simple @readonly label. This not only saves time and reduces the chance of errors but is easy to read and therefore debug. This is just one example of what decorators can do.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"the-beginning---objects-and-properties\\">The Beginning - Objects and Properties</h2>\\n<p>We will start with creating basic objects and giving them properties, this will help understand how decorators work as they are built on how this is implemented under the hood. Creating objects and adding properties has always been exceptionally easy in javascript due to its dynamic nature, the most basic way is shown below:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> myObj = {\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">message</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Hello Universe\\"</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">show</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>) </span>{\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">console</span>.log(<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">this</span>.message);\\n    }\\n};\\n\\nmyObj.show();\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// =&gt; Hello Universe</span></code></pre>\\n<p>In ES5 several things were introduced to make properties of an object have more complex behaviour of their own with <code>Object.defineProperty</code>. I will just cover the basics here but I recommend reading the <a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en/docs/Web/javascript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/defineProperty\\">MDN article</a> to get more details on how they work.</p>\\n<p><code>Object.defineProperty(target, prop, descriptor)</code> receives a target which is the object that you want create a property on, a prop which is a string with the name of the new property and a descriptor. The descriptor is an object containing behaviour for the new property including its initial value. Value can be any valid javascript variable you want, including functions:</p>\\n<p>The simplest format is:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = {\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">question</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Meaning of life, the Universe and Everything?\\"</span>\\n};\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> prop = <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'answer\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> descriptor = {\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">value</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">42</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">writable</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">enumerable</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>,\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">configurable</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>\\n};\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">Object</span>.defineProperty(obj, prop, descriptor);\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Which is functionally equivalent to:</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = {\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">answer</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">42</span>\\n};</code></pre>\\n<p>Obviously the later is much less verbose, but in this example we could of made <code>writable: false</code> then the value could not be changed later on, effectively making the property &#39;readonly&#39;. You can see the advantages of <code>Object.defineProperty</code> when this level of control is needed, even if it is very bloated to type. This is not easy to read and therefore would be horrible to debug, fortunately we can use decorators to do a lot of this heavy lifting for us.</p>\\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> When using the less verbose version above, javascript implements the longer version under the hood, meaning that it will have a descriptor even though you didnt explicitly use one. That descriptor can be retrieved using the <code>Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor</code> method.</em></p>\\n<h2 id=\\"decorators-1\\">Decorators</h2>\\n<p>So what has <code>Object.defineProperty</code> got to do with decorators? When we define a decorator we create a function that has the same arguments as <code>Object.defineProperty</code> which is intentional as it allows us to effectively &#39;wrap&#39; <code>Object.defineProperty</code> with other functionality that is run before that <code>Object.defineProperty</code>, and best of all it doesnt require the more verbose syntax of using <code>Object.defineProperty</code>:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">readonly</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">target, prop, descriptor</span>) </span>{\\n    descriptor.writable = <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">false</span>;\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> descriptor;\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = {\\n    @readonly\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">answer</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">42</span>\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj2 = {\\n    @readonly\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">question</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"Meaning of life, the Universe and Everything?\\"</span>\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p>This is not only much easier to read, but saves repeating the code you write. You can also use multiple decorators at once, so you can have focused decorators rather than have to mix functionality:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">nonconfigurable</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">target, key, descriptor</span>) </span>{\\n    descriptor.configurable = <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">false</span>;\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> descriptor;\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = {\\n    @readonly\\n    @nonconfigurable\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">answer</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">42</span>\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"decorators-with-parameters\\">Decorators With Parameters</h2>\\n<p>As a decorator is just a function, you can wrap it in another function that returns the decorator function.</p>\\n<p>These are sometimes referred to as higher-order functions (a function that accepts another function(s) as an argument or returns a function based on the arguments passed to it). The advantage of that means we can use decorators with parameters that feed data into the decorator:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">readonly</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">isReadonly</span>) </span>{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> (<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">target, prop, descriptor</span>) </span>{\\n        descriptor.writable = !isReadonly;\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> descriptor;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = {\\n    @readonly(<span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">true</span>)\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">answer</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">42</span>\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"classes\\">Classes</h2>\\n<p>More typically rather than using object literals examples show ES5 classes, below shows using the core-decorators <code>@deprecate</code> on an ES5 class function:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-class\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span> </span>{\\n\\n    @deprecate\\n    myFunc() {\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<p><em><strong>Note:</strong> In earlier examples we have used @readonly to decorate data properties, however ES5 classes do not have data properties. They, like decorators, appear to be coming in ES2016 (ES7) to enable them in babel you would use <a href=\\"https://babeljs.io/docs/plugins/transform-class-properties/\\">babel-plugin-transform-class-properties</a> which is available on <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/babel-plugin-transform-class-properties\\">NPM</a>.</em></p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-class\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span> </span>{\\n\\n    @readonly\\n    myProp = <span class=\\"hljs-number\\">100</span>;\\n}</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"class-decorators\\">Class Decorators</h2>\\n<p>As well as the properties/functions of a class, decorators can be added to ES5 class definitions too. The only difference being that the decorator method only receives the first argument &#39;target&#39;, which refers to the class definition. This doesnt give us access to the objects being created, but it does give us access to the prototype from which those objects will be created:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">logging</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">target</span>) </span>{\\n    target.prototype.log = <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">message</span>) </span>{\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">console</span>.log(message);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n@logging\\n<span class=\\"hljs-class\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span> </span>{\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass();\\nobj.log(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'Hello World\'</span>);\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// =&gt; Hello World</span></code></pre>\\n<p>Like with properties you can wrap and return the function to pass it data or functions, that could be different for each class. The following is just a basic example to show how it works:</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">logging</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">logger</span>) </span>{\\n    target.prototype.log = logger;\\n}\\n\\n@logging(<span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">console</span>.log)\\n<span class=\\"hljs-class\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">MyClass</span></span>{\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> obj = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> MyClass();\\nobj.log(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'Hello World\'</span>);</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"advanced\\">Advanced</h2>\\n<p>I wont go into some of the more advanced uses, but I do recommend you looking at some of the great articles and code already out there. In particular I recommend the following article on <a href=\\"http://raganwald.com/2015/06/26/decorators-in-es7.html\\">mixins</a> and looking through the source code on <a href=\\"https://github.com/jayphelps/core-decorators.js\\">core-decorators</a> which shows various ways of manipulating the target or descriptor.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"summary\\">Summary</h2>\\n<p>Decorators are a great way of adding reuseable code to existing classes to give some kind of different functionality. Although the spec will change slightly the concept will be the same and adds a useful feature to the language that will also make it easier for us to read/debug.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"references\\">References</h2>\\n<ul>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://medium.com/google-developers/exploring-es7-decorators-76ecb65fb841\\">https://medium.com/google-developers/exploring-es7-decorators-76ecb65fb841</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://github.com/wycats/javascript-decorators\\">https://github.com/wycats/javascript-decorators</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://github.com/jayphelps/core-decorators.js\\">https://github.com/jayphelps/core-decorators.js</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"http://raganwald.com/2015/06/26/decorators-in-es7.html\\">http://raganwald.com/2015/06/26/decorators-in-es7.html</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en/docs/Web/javascript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/defineProperty\\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en/docs/Web/javascript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/defineProperty</a></li>\\n<li><a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/getOwnPropertyDescriptor\\">https://developer.mozilla.org/en/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/getOwnPropertyDescriptor</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["javascript","es2015","es2016","es2016","classes"]}')}}]);